3.text:
The four clauses are a componental description of the three-box / three-sun grid, not a literal stroke inventory.

一横又一横再一横 = the three horizontal bars top-middle-bottom that close the three 日 / the three bars of the grid;
一竖又一竖再一竖 = the three left-hand uprights of the three 日;
一竖在左一竖在右 = the left/right framing pair of a 日/口 compartment, i.e. the sides that delimit the middle 日;
一竖又一竖再一竖 = the three right-hand uprights of the three 日.

That is 日 日 日 placed side by side.

In rhetoric that is the 12-stroke block 晶 jīng ‘bright, crystal’, literally ‘three suns’. 
The same 3 H + 6 V grouping is taught in elementary stroke talk as the three-bar grid 品 pǐn / 丰 fēng, the formal orthographic realisation of the pattern.

The poem is the classic 晶 / 品 three-horizontal with side-verticals riddle. The conventional name under which the verse is quoted is 晶, with 品 as its elementary three-storey grid form. 

帶, 帚/曲, 林/森 all require hooks, cover strokes or asymmetric components that the clue never mentions; the clue stays on pure horizontals and verticals and on the explicit left-right framing of a 日 enclosure, which is exactly the three-日 grid.

