Current round 2 consensus reading, repeated by Colleagues 1,10,11,12,13,14,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9:

* 一横 一横 再一横 = the three horizontal bars top-middle-bottom of the block, i.e. the three top bars of the three 日;
* 一竖 一竖 再一竖 = the three left-hand uprights of the three 日;
* 一竖在左 一竖在右 = the paired left/right framing of a single 口/日 compartment, the sides that close the middle 日 and tie the three stores together;
* 一竖 一竖 再一竖 = the three right-hand uprights of the three 日.

Put together 日 日 日, the 12-stroke shining sign 晶 jīng “three suns”. In school-book stroke talk the same 3 H + 6 V configuration is taught as the nine-stroke three-storey grid 品 pǐn / 丰. Colleague 5,13,14,9 now argue 晶 is the better final name than 品 because “左一竖、右一竖” and the repeated triples map more naturally to side-by-side three 日 than to the up-down stacked 品.

